#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96f54294-8bb4-5338-969b-76c8d1536daa" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Law of Total Probability in Terms of Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63016c40-26b8-54f1-af02-fe4df536e028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.044" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436173} {:answer/id #uuid "9e6154ce-f843-5fdc-a8b0-5dfb33599b4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.058" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436185} {:answer/id #uuid "cc67574e-605f-521d-93f0-a43b7eb005d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.541" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436195} {:answer/id #uuid "e6bab3ee-bcc0-51a4-967f-ae1ffb30e505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.056" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436204} {:answer/id #uuid "efcedd14-6e3b-5303-8273-c5f15c5397fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.054" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63016c40-26b8-54f1-af02-fe4df536e028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.044" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436173} :answer-field/id #uuid "efcaebab-808b-586a-b686-8d0602967092" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "296a9d4d-087e-5552-b718-aa436d5fbac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-143748" :question/problem "A company produces replacement batteries for a particular cell phone model at three locations. Location $A$ produces $30%$ of the batteries, of which $3%$ are defective. Location $B$ produces $38%$ of the batteries, of which $5%$ are defective. And location $C$  produces $32%$ of the batteries, of which also $5%$ are defective. What is the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that the battery is defective, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the battery was produced at location $A,$\n\n- let $B$  be the event that the battery was produced at location $B,$\n\n- let $C$  be the event that the battery was produced at location $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since  location $A$ produces $30%$ of the batteries,  location $B$ produces $38%$ of the batteries, and location $C$ produces $32%$ of the batteries, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.3,\\,P(B)=0.38,\\,P(C)=0.32.\n$$\n\n- Since $3%$ of the batteries produced at location $A$, $5%$ of the batteries produced at location $B$, and $5%$ of the batteries produced at  location $C,$ are defective, we have \n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.03,\\,P(D|B)=0.05,\\,P(D|C)=0.05.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected battery is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.03)(0.3)+(0.05)(0.38)+(0.05)(0.32) \\\\  & =0.009+0.019+0.016 \\\\  & =0.044.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092315 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96f54294-8bb4-5338-969b-76c8d1536daa" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Law of Total Probability in Terms of Conditional Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14a66e4d-d36b-5589-9e76-8d2fc1124283" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.525" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436146} {:answer/id #uuid "32133a22-82bc-5a7c-9eac-727b8484e3b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.515" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436154} {:answer/id #uuid "357f442f-a27d-5c2b-8c03-4173f9ed1135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.555" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436158} {:answer/id #uuid "35872c71-32ca-5629-8a73-9868fcb5d5a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.625" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436159} {:answer/id #uuid "82363ae0-ea2c-5ea2-bd36-f1a97cf797bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.575" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82363ae0-ea2c-5ea2-bd36-f1a97cf797bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.575" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436178} :answer-field/id #uuid "e132532a-be97-5775-bb17-2406d8caeef2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436218}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c588063-b6d3-5525-ae01-ed797a4d9cc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-143753" :question/problem "In a gas station, $40%$ of the clients buy Regular gasoline, $45%$ buy Premium gasoline, and $15%$ buy Supreme gasoline. If $65%$ of the clients that purchase Regular gasoline paid with a credit card, $50%$ of the clients that buy Premium gasoline paid with a credit card, and $60%$ of the clients that buy Supreme gasoline paid with a credit card,  what is the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card?" :question/worked-solution "Let  $C$ be the event that the client paid with a credit card, and\n\n- let $R$ be the event that the client bought Regular gasoline,\n\n- let $Q$  be the event that the client bought Premium gasoline,\n\n- let $S$ be the event that the client bought Supreme gasoline.\n\nThen the disjoint union $R⊔Q⊔S$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C\\cap R)+P(C\\cap Q)+P(C\\cap S) \\\\  & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|Q)P(Q)+P(C|S)P(S)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40%$ of the clients buy Regular gasoline, $45%$ of the clients buy Premium gasoline, and $15%$ of the clients buy Supreme gasoline, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.4,\\,P(Q)=0.45,\\,P(S)=0.15.\n$$\n\n- Since $65%$ of the clients that buy Regular gasoline, $50%$ of the clients that buy Premium gasoline, and $60%$ of the clients that buy Supreme gasoline,  paid with a credit card, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|R)=0.65,\\,P(C|Q)=0.5,\\,P(C|S)=0.6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|Q)P(Q)+P(C|S)P(S) \\\\  & =(0.65)(0.4)+(0.5)(0.45)+(0.6)(0.15) \\\\  & =0.26+0.225+0.009 \\\\  & =0.575.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186436224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092316 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0ebe700-63af-57d4-ae8f-9e09dac5cf76" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Using the Law of Total Probability"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49d67ba6-1b1f-58fa-a1c0-e61e944c3560" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.044" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436163} {:answer/id #uuid "82276f72-0877-5798-8fe1-76ed94a97b68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.045" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436177} {:answer/id #uuid "9482cc37-4f52-55f0-820b-76e7d8d26527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.038" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436182} {:answer/id #uuid "e4fcf1de-ebb1-592d-9f04-b9c050c3551b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0052" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436203} {:answer/id #uuid "ea852479-a868-5154-ba52-8c97031f5080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea852479-a868-5154-ba52-8c97031f5080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.04" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436205} :answer-field/id #uuid "e674252e-02a4-53ae-adde-553bbd0c47c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186436219}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "199cacb7-9e7a-5d07-af35-fc181af78213" :question/math-academy-id "q-143872" :question/problem "A company manufactures electronic components. Of the component population, $45%$ are produced by machine $A,$  $25%$ by machine $B,$ and $30%$ by machine $C.$ If $5%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ and $3%$ of those produced by machine $B$ are defective, and the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is $0.042,$ what is the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $C$ is defective?" :question/worked-solution "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly selected component is defective, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the component was produced by machine $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap B)+P(D\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation. \n\n- Since machine $A$ produces $45%$ of the components, machine $B$ produces $25%$ of the components, and machine $C$ produces $30%$ of the components, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.45,\\,P(B)=0.25,\\,P(C)=0.3.\n$$\n\n- Since $5%$ of the components produced by machine $A$ and $3%$ of the components produced by machine $B$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.05,\\,P(D|B)=0.03\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is $0.042,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=0.042.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(D|C),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|B)P(B)+P(D|C)P(C) \\\\ 0.042 & =(0.05)(0.45)+(0.03)(0.25)+P(D|C)(0.3) \\\\ 0.042 & =0.0225+0.0075+0.3P(D|C) \\\\ 0.3P(D|C) & =0.012 \\\\ P(D|C) & =0.04\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected component produced by machine $C$ is defective is $0.04.$"}]}
